   "provenance": [
    {
     "note": "20 °C value 79 W/mK for RT9010 (mitsubishi-2026-tm102g p. 4; zhang-2025 Tab. 2 uses 88 for H13A); 30 % loss RT->1000 °C after vornberger-2020-carbide-hot-hardness, linear in T",
     "src": "mitsubishi-2026-tm102g",
     "what": "tool material carbide-k20-6co thermal properties"
    },
    {
     "note": "Jaspers & Dautzenberg (2002) constants as stated by Burns et al. 2012 Eq. (1.2), with Tr = 20 C and Tf = 1490 C; also used by xiu-2019-1045-grinding-fe Table 4. Burns et al. report that FE simulations with this set under-predict the peak tool-chip temperature by hundreds of C and that rapidly heated 1045 is stiffer below the eutectoid (723 C). Alternative: Murugesan & Jung 2019 (Materials 12:609) fitted A 50.1, B 176.1 for hot forming at 1223 K reference, not suitable for cutting. The Jaspers & Dautzenberg original (J. Mater. Process. Technol., 2002) could not be read in the second research round either (paywalled; no open thesis copy found), so the set is confirmed only through Burns et al. and xiu-2019-1045-grinding-fe.",
     "src": "burns-2012-1045-cutting-temperature",
     "what": "aisi1045 flow stress (Johnson-Cook)"
    },
    {
     "note": "20 C; Table 1 gives 7850 (20 C) falling to 7649 (1000 C)",
